 Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
 
-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+### 2Q Eviction
+
+The cache uses 2Q eviction so a long sequential screening cannot evict the sheets editors repeatedly compare. It maintains two live queues:
+
+- **First-use queue** — sheets admitted after their first render. New entries enter at the tail.
+- **Reused queue** — sheets that have proven reuse. A cache hit on a first-use sheet promotes it to the tail of the reused queue. A cache hit on a reused sheet moves it to the tail.
+
+The first-use queue is capped at a configurable fraction of the 12 GiB budget; the reused queue uses the remainder. This split is a tuning parameter and does not affect the external contract. The design adds no ghost queues: protection comes from the two live queues, and a discarded sheet is simply re-rendered on a later miss.
+
+Eviction follows these rules:
+
+- When admitting a sheet would exceed the first-use queue's cap, evict the head of the first-use queue.
+- When admitting a sheet would exceed the 12 GiB budget, evict from the head of the first-use queue until the first-use queue is empty or the budget is satisfied; evict from the head of the reused queue only when the first-use queue has no entries left to evict.
+- A reused sheet therefore outlives first-use sheets while the first-use queue still has entries. When the reused queue alone fills the budget, admitting a new first-use sheet evicts from the reused queue; the accepted cost is the extra queue and the split parameter.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Cache eviction policy | 2Q with a first-use queue and a reused queue | LRU; frequency-based eviction with aging | LRU lets a long sequential screening evict the sheets editors compare. Aged frequency counts add maintenance and retain yesterday's favorites after a project switch. 2Q keeps reused sheets through screening scans while confining all bookkeeping to the preview worker. |
+| Queue history | Two live queues, no ghost queues | Full 2Q with ghost queues | Ghost history adds bookkeeping without changing the protection goal; a discarded sheet is re-rendered on a later miss. |
+| Reused-queue ordering | Recency: a hit moves the sheet to the tail | FIFO insertion order | Recency keeps repeatedly popular comparison sheets at the protected end of the queue. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+
+1. ✅ Cache eviction policy is 2Q (see Decisions & Alternatives).
+
+### Deferred
+
+1. First-use queue share of the 12 GiB budget. A starting point of 3 GiB (25%) is plausible; tune it against the observed mix of long screenings and comparison sessions.
+2. Whether first-use churn ever justifies a ghost history to avoid re-rendering comparison sheets that miss before their second access.
